# Line-ending policy, deliberately narrow: only `*.sh` is declared, so adding
# this file renormalizes one file instead of the whole tree.
#
# bash cannot parse a CRLF script — it fails with
#   syntax error near unexpected token `$'do\r''
# — so `install.sh` was unrunnable on Linux while it was committed with CRLF
# endings, which made the documented `curl … install.sh | bash` path dead on
# arrival. Shell scripts are now always LF, on every platform, including a
# Windows checkout that is only editing them.
*.sh text eol=lf

# `install.ps1` is intentionally NOT declared here. It targets Windows and is
# already committed with CRLF; adding a `text eol=crlf` rule would normalize the
# stored blob to LF and rewrite every line of the file in the index for no
# behavioral gain.
